      "output": {
        "claims": [
          {
            "key": "croatian_vs_usual_logical_approaches",
            "text": "The Croatian group's approach to machine translation was different from the usual logical approaches of the period and advocated cybernetic methods that the mainstream AI community adopted only decades later.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1908.08917",
                "chunk_id": "c-abb668d90226308be8e33e5f"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "ussr_four_approaches",
            "text": "In the USSR there were four major approaches to machine translation in the late 1950s; the information-theoretic approach of Lyapunov, Kulagina and Dobrushin was considered cybernetic at that time and served as the main role model for the Croatian efforts from 1957 onward.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1908.08917",
                "chunk_id": "c-bd28c872a0ec084173d1c794"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "lagging_couple_years",
            "text": "The authors conclude that the poorly funded Croatian research was lagging only a couple of years behind the research of the superpowers, which invested heavily in this effort.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1908.08917",
                "chunk_id": "c-4fb7583763707533165c64d2"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "no_computer",
            "text": "Unlike their colleagues in the USA and the USSR, Laszlo's group had to manage without an actual computer, and its results remained mainly theoretical.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1908.08917",
                "chunk_id": "c-4fb7583763707533165c64d2"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "us_ussr_computers_suboptimal_results",
            "text": "Both the USA and the USSR had access to state-of-the-art computers with political support making them available to machine translation researchers, but results were poor in the late 1950s and all work was theoretical work implemented on a computer, which proved sub-optimal.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1908.08917",
                "chunk_id": "c-cecf76c7b16c45dc2fcf28e9"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "croatian_state_of_art_references",
            "text": "Laszlo and Petrović noted USA prototype efforts from 1954, a 1955 collection of research papers, USSR efforts starting from 1955 and a UK prototype from 1956, but they do not detail or cite the articles they mention.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1908.08917",
                "chunk_id": "c-4fb7583763707533165c64d2"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "military_priority_abroad",
            "text": "Laszlo and Petrović noted that research in computational linguistics was mainly a top-priority military effort in other countries.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1908.08917",
                "chunk_id": "c-4fb7583763707533165c64d2"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decision": "answer"
